import{_ as a,o as s,c as t,a6 as i}from"./chunks/framework.CCKCzdWs.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"Upgrading","description":"What happens when a new Piwi version starts, how to upgrade safely, why there is no downgrade, and what to do when an upgrade goes wrong.","frontmatter":{"title":"Upgrading","description":"What happens when a new Piwi version starts, how to upgrade safely, why there is no downgrade, and what to do when an upgrade goes wrong.","lang":"en-US","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://piwitests.dev/operate/upgrading.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://piwitests.dev/operate/upgrading.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Upgrading | Piwi for Playwright"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":"What happens when a new Piwi version starts, how to upgrade safely, why there is no downgrade, and what to do when an upgrade goes wrong."}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"operate/upgrading.md","filePath":"operate/upgrading.md","lastUpdated":1795619799000}'),n={name:"operate/upgrading.md"};function r(o,e,h,l,d,p){return s(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i(`<h1 id="upgrading" tabindex="-1">Upgrading <a class="header-anchor" href="#upgrading" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upgrading&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Piwi is pre-1.0. Minor releases can carry breaking changes, and the database schema moves with them. Upgrading is normally a one-line change — but it&#39;s a one-way door, so it&#39;s worth knowing what happens before you pull a new tag.</p><h2 id="the-short-version" tabindex="-1">The short version <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-short-version" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The short version&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li>Read the <a href="https://github.com/PiwiTests/piwi/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">changelog</a> for the versions you&#39;re skipping, looking for <strong>⚠ BREAKING CHANGES</strong>.</li><li><strong>Wait until nothing is in progress</strong> (<a href="#wait-for-the-runs-in-progress">what to wait for</a>): a run that ends while the server is down loses its end.</li><li><strong>Back up</strong> the database and file storage (<a href="./backup-restore.html">how</a>).</li><li>Pull the new tag and restart.</li><li>Check the logs for <code>migrations completed successfully</code>, then confirm the version at <strong>Settings → About</strong>.</li></ol><h2 id="wait-for-the-runs-in-progress" tabindex="-1">Wait for the runs in progress <a class="header-anchor" href="#wait-for-the-runs-in-progress" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Wait for the runs in progress&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A restart cuts what is in flight: the end of a run, an upload, an AI diagnosis, an assistant answer. <strong>Settings → About</strong> shows an administrator the runs in progress. A script asks the instance with an administrator&#39;s API key:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">node</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;fetch(&#39;http://localhost:3000/api/admin/activity&#39;,{headers:{&#39;X-API-Key&#39;:&#39;pd_your_key&#39;}}).then(r=&gt;r.text()).then(console.log)&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>Nothing is left to wait for once <code>runsInProgress</code> is empty and <code>requestsInProgress</code>, <code>diagnosesInProgress</code> and <code>assistantAnswersInProgress</code> are 0. <code>openEventStreams</code> is for information: browser tabs, live views and connected apps reconnect on their own. The work a run&#39;s end leaves running in the background (notifications, issue tracker automation, pull-request feedback, auto-heal) is not counted.</p><p>A streaming reporter is heard at least every 15 seconds, so a run whose <code>lastActivityAt</code> is minutes old was probably abandoned, its CI job cancelled. The instance interrupts it 2 minutes after its last activity, or after an hour for a run in its global setup (<code>initializing</code>) or a sharded run waiting for a shard, which stay quiet while they wait. It settles a <code>finalizing</code> run after 10 minutes.</p><h2 id="what-happens-when-a-new-version-starts" tabindex="-1">What happens when a new version starts <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-happens-when-a-new-version-starts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What happens when a new version starts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On boot, before any request can touch the database (requests that need it wait for these steps):</p><ol><li><strong>Database migrations run automatically</strong> — SQLite or PostgreSQL, whichever you&#39;re on. There is no separate migrate command and nothing to run by hand. Before migrating, Piwi compares the migrations the database has recorded with the ones this version ships (see <a href="./database.html#a-database-that-ran-another-build">A database that ran another build</a>).</li><li><strong>Failure clusters are re-fingerprinted</strong> if the fingerprint algorithm changed in this release. This is non-destructive: existing clusters are updated in place, and clusters that now collide are merged into the one first seen earliest rather than dropped, so your triage statuses and notes survive. Clusters merged earlier stay merged, and tests moved to a cluster of their own stay there.</li></ol><p>Step 2 logs an error and continues if it fails. <strong>Step 1 does not.</strong> If a migration fails, the process keeps running but every database call fails with the migration error and <code>GET /api/health</code> returns <code>503</code>, so the container reports unhealthy — nothing is served against a half-migrated schema. That&#39;s deliberate: a loud failure you can restore from beats silent corruption.</p><p>On the upgrade that adds project roles, a migration turns each Reporter account into a Member with the Maintainer role on the projects it could open, and each User account into a Member with the Viewer role, so nobody gains or loses a right (see <a href="./project-access.html#project-roles">Project access</a>). A database from before project access existed gets the same roles on all projects, as that upgrade granted.</p><p>On the upgrade that makes email addresses unique ignoring case, a migration first looks for users whose addresses differ only by letter case (<code>Bob@corp.com</code> and <code>bob@corp.com</code>), such as a second account an OAuth sign-in created for someone who already had one. Piwi never merges accounts on its own: if it finds any, the migration fails like any other (see above) with an error that says so, lists the user ids on PostgreSQL, and changes nothing. Fix it in the database, then restart:</p><div class="language-sql vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sql</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">-- The users that share an address</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SELECT</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> id, username, email </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">FROM</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> users</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">WHERE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> lower</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(email) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">IN</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> (</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SELECT</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> lower</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(email) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">FROM</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> users </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">GROUP BY</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> lower</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(email) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">HAVING</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> count</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">*</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">);</span></span>
<span class="line"></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">-- For every user but one of each address</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">UPDATE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> users </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">SET</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> email </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> NULL</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, email_verified </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 0</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> WHERE</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> id </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">id</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>When one is an account an OAuth sign-in created by mistake, clear its address, then delete it from <strong>Settings → Users</strong> once the server runs: the person&#39;s next sign-in with that provider links their original account, if it verified its address.</p><p>The same upgrade refuses invite, password reset and verification links emailed before it: send them again.</p><h2 id="downgrading-is-not-supported" tabindex="-1">Downgrading is not supported <a class="header-anchor" href="#downgrading-is-not-supported" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Downgrading is not supported&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Migrations are <strong>forward-only</strong> — there are no down migrations. Once a new version has migrated your database, an older version will not run against it correctly, and starting one may make things worse.</p><p>So the rollback path is not &quot;pull the old tag&quot;, it&#39;s <strong>restore your backup</strong>:</p><ol><li>Stop the container.</li><li>Restore the database and <code>.data/storage/</code> from the backup pair you took before upgrading, as <a href="./backup-restore.html#restoring">Backup &amp; restore → Restoring</a> describes.</li><li>Start the previous version&#39;s tag, with the same <code>PIWI_SECRET_KEY</code>.</li></ol><p>This is the entire reason step 3 of the short version isn&#39;t optional.</p><h2 id="pin-a-version" tabindex="-1">Pin a version <a class="header-anchor" href="#pin-a-version" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pin a version&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Running <code>latest</code> in production means an unattended <code>docker pull</code> can move you across a breaking change. Pin the exact version and bump it deliberately:</p><div class="language-yaml vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">yaml</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># docker-compose.yml</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">services</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">  piwi</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">:</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#22863A;--shiki-dark:#85E89D;">    image</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">: </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">phenx/piwitests-server:0.26.1</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # not :latest</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The available tag patterns — and the GHCR mirror — are in <a href="./deployment.html#available-tags">Deployment → Available tags</a>.</p><h2 id="verifying-the-upgrade-landed" tabindex="-1">Verifying the upgrade landed <a class="header-anchor" href="#verifying-the-upgrade-landed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verifying the upgrade landed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Three ways, in increasing order of automation:</p><ul><li><p><strong>Settings → About</strong> shows the running version, the build SHA, the Node.js version, the database backend, the operating system, and the latest run&#39;s reporter and Playwright versions.</p></li><li><p>The version endpoint returns the version, build SHA, database backend, platform and architecture as JSON, along with the Node.js version actually running — no authentication required:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">curl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -s</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> http://localhost:3000/api/version</span></span></code></pre></div></li><li><p><code>GET /api/health</code> is the readiness probe — it verifies database connectivity and returns 503 when the database isn&#39;t reachable, which is what you want a container orchestrator watching.</p></li></ul><p>In the startup logs, the lines worth grepping for are <code>Running &lt;dialect&gt; migrations from …</code> and <code>migrations completed successfully</code> — plus <code>Migration history repaired</code> after a <a href="./database.html#a-database-that-ran-another-build">repair</a>.</p><h2 id="upgrading-the-reporter" tabindex="-1">Upgrading the reporter <a class="header-anchor" href="#upgrading-the-reporter" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upgrading the reporter&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The reporter and the dashboard version independently, and you do <strong>not</strong> have to upgrade them in lockstep:</p><ul><li>A <strong>newer reporter against an older server</strong> degrades gracefully — if the server doesn&#39;t understand streaming, the reporter falls back to batch submission on its own.</li><li>An <strong>older reporter against a newer server</strong> keeps working; wire fields are added, not repurposed.</li></ul><p>That said, features land in pairs. Locator healing needs both a reporter that captures snapshots and a server that ranks them, so if a new capability doesn&#39;t appear, matching the two versions is the first thing to try.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">npm</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> install</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --save-dev</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> @piwitests/reporter@latest</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="upgrading-the-desktop-app" tabindex="-1">Upgrading the desktop app <a class="header-anchor" href="#upgrading-the-desktop-app" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Upgrading the desktop app&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <a href="/features/desktop.html">desktop build</a> bundles its own server, so installing a newer build upgrades both. Its data is migrated on first launch under the same forward-only rule: back it up first, as <a href="./backup-restore.html#the-desktop-app">Backup &amp; restore → The desktop app</a> describes. On Windows an in-app update closes the app as soon as the download finishes and hands over to the installer, which reopens it when done. The <code>.exe</code> installer runs without a window; the <code>.msi</code> one shows a progress bar.</p><h2 id="if-an-upgrade-goes-wrong" tabindex="-1">If an upgrade goes wrong <a class="header-anchor" href="#if-an-upgrade-goes-wrong" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;If an upgrade goes wrong&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>The container is unhealthy after upgrading</strong> (<code>/api/health</code> returns <code>503</code> and anything that reads data fails). Check the logs for <code>Migration error</code>. The schema is mid-flight or incompatible; restore your backup and open an <a href="https://github.com/PiwiTests/piwi/issues" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">issue</a> with the error. If the error starts with <code>Migration history repair failed</code>, the database ran another build and could not be brought in line automatically (<a href="./database.html#a-database-that-ran-another-build">details</a>); it was left as it was.</p><p><strong>Every page answers <code>403</code> and lists the dashboard&#39;s local addresses.</strong> With authentication off, name the hosts clients use, a container&#39;s name included, in <code>PIWI_SITE_URL</code> or <code>PIWI_ALLOWED_HOSTS</code>, or turn authentication on (<a href="./authentication.html#disabling-authentication">why</a>).</p><p><strong>The dashboard loads but data looks wrong.</strong> Don&#39;t downgrade — restore the backup instead, then report what you saw. Downgrading on a migrated database compounds the problem.</p><p><strong>The Windows desktop installer says &quot;Error opening file for writing&quot;.</strong> Up to version 0.37, an in-app update could leave the app&#39;s bundled server running — a <code>node.exe</code> in the install folder, <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Piwi Dashboard</code> for the <code>.exe</code> — and the installer could not replace the files it holds. Installers from later versions stop it first. If you hit it, don&#39;t choose <strong>Ignore</strong>, which leaves an old file behind: in Task Manager&#39;s <strong>Details</strong> tab, end the <code>node.exe</code> whose <strong>Open file location</strong> is that folder, then choose <strong>Retry</strong>.</p><p><strong>Failure clusters look reorganized.</strong> Expected after a fingerprint-algorithm change: clusters that now share a root cause have merged. Triage state is carried across the merge. A cluster that held unrelated failures under one <a href="/guide/concepts.html#error-fingerprint-failure-cluster">generic error</a> keeps them, and their next failures join a cluster per file they fail in.</p><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="./backup-restore.html">Backup &amp; restore</a> — what to back up and how</li><li><a href="./deployment.html#available-tags">Deployment → Available tags</a> — what to pin</li><li><a href="https://github.com/PiwiTests/piwi/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Changelog</a> — breaking changes per release</li></ul>`,46)])])}const u=a(n,[["render",r]]);export{c as __pageData,u as default};
